{"schema":"uos.t23-independent-dev-acceptance.v1","round":2,"reviewer":"claude-independent-verifier-r2","plan_id":"20261029-1913-mcp-server-completion","task_id":"T23","observed_at":"2026-10-30T11:07:22Z","parent_main_commit":"b5dfbe78d0fec1211ba08c0f3bb019fbb4c573ae","core_dev_ready":true,"mcp_full_dev_ready":false,"system_admission":"NOT_ADMITTED","raw_verdict":"HOLD","critical_findings_count":2,"critical_findings":[{"id":"T23-R2-C01","category":"acceptance_evidence","location":"apps/cepaf_gleam/test/mcp_client_matrix_test.gleam:49,732 and apps/cepaf_gleam/src/cepaf_gleam/mcp/supervisor.gleam:92","finding":"The claimed \"535 positive candidate-bound cells\" are positive-outcome-bound but NOT candidate-bound in the two-key sense. Every candidate value in the acceptance chain is a hardcoded build-invariant literal, never a revision identifier: the test pins `const matrix_candidate = \"uos-mcp\"` (line 49) and the sole acceptance assertion requires `cell.candidate == matrix_candidate` (line 732, comment claims \"the true active candidate\"); the test's store sets `active_candidate: matrix_candidate` (line 135) and ledger `new_ledger(matrix_candidate)`; production `supervisor.gleam:92` builds the live worker-pool ledger with the constant `\"uos-mcp-candidate\"`; and every public adapter hardcodes `\"uos-mcp\"` as its evidence candidate (analysis.gleam, knowledge.gleam, planning_read.gleam, planning_write.gleam). Nothing in the chain binds to parent_main_commit b5dfbe78d0fec1211ba08c0f3bb019fbb4c573ae, a jj change id, or any source digest. This is the unremediated residue of Codex T23-C04, whose required correction was \"positive, candidate-bound outcomes ... for every required cell\".","verification":"I executed mcp_client_matrix_test myself against a verified-fresh build (every remediated .beam mtime >= its .gleam mtime; sources modified 10:55-11:01Z, ~71 min before my run). All 4 tests pass, including 535 cells. I then read the assertion: the predicate is satisfied by construction because the asserted constant is the same literal the production adapters emit. Falsifier: the assertion is revision-invariant — it would pass byte-identically at the pre-remediation parent commit and at any future revision, so it cannot discharge candidate binding. Positive progress confirmed and credited: candidate substitution IS removed (candidate_from_frame returns \"\" on absent evidence, which fails the assertion rather than being replaced), frames now require result && !error && isError!=true (frame_is_positive_result, lines 321-335), and arguments are meaningful per-family (positive_args_for, lines 356-449). Those three sub-defects of C04 are genuinely fixed; only the candidate-binding property is not.","required_correction":"Derive the active candidate from an actual revision identifier (jj change/commit id or a source-tree digest) in supervisor.gleam and in the acceptance fixture, assert cell.candidate equals that revision, and add a negative control in which a foreign/stale revision fails the cell. Until then the matrix must be reported as \"535 positive cells, candidate binding NOT established\"."},{"id":"T23-R2-C02","category":"evidence_integrity","location":"apps/cepaf_gleam/src/cepaf_gleam/mcp/route_convergence.gleam:459-468 and 434-453","finding":"The production candidate-mismatch guard contains a constant-string escape hatch and then manufactures candidate binding by relabeling. `candidate_evidence_mismatch` (line 464-467) returns False — i.e. ACCEPTS — when the emitted evidence candidate is the empty string OR the literal \"uos-mcp\", because the conjunction short-circuits on `evidence_candidate != \"\" && evidence_candidate != \"uos-mcp\"`. On acceptance, `run_converged` (lines 443-452) rewrites the record with `candidate: store.active_candidate`. Consequently an edge handler or adapter that emits NO candidate evidence, or the generic product literal, yields a `Complete` whose EvidenceRecord affirmatively claims the active candidate. The guard therefore only catches evidence that names a third, explicitly different candidate; it cannot catch absent or generic candidate evidence, which is the common case since every public adapter hardcodes \"uos-mcp\".","verification":"Source-bound, traced by hand through both branches. For evidence_candidate = \"\": the first conjunct `\"\" != \"\"` is False, so candidate_evidence_mismatch returns False, so the Complete branch is taken and the EvidenceRecord is rewritten to store.active_candidate. For evidence_candidate = \"uos-mcp\": the second conjunct is False, same path. Only a fourth distinct string reaches CANDIDATE_EVIDENCE_MISMATCH. I did not observe a privilege escalation and none is claimed — the defect is evidential, not an authority bypass: it converts \"no candidate observed\" into \"active candidate asserted\".","required_correction":"Fail closed on absent candidate evidence (empty string must refuse, not pass), remove the \"uos-mcp\" literal whitelist, and stop rewriting the candidate field on the success path — verify the emitted candidate against the active candidate instead of overwriting it. Add negative controls for empty-candidate and generic-candidate evidence."}],"minor_observations":["REMEDIATIONS I INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED BY EXECUTION, not by reading the remediation claim: I ran 74 tests across 13 suites on a verified-fresh build, all passing — mcp_route_parity_test (7), mcp_worker_recovery_test (7), mcp_client_matrix_test (4), mcp_workspace_disposition_test (3), mcp_final_acceptance_test (4), mcp_assurance_test (5), mcp_transport_test (5), mcp_docs_truth_test (3) = 38/38; plus mcp_authz_wiring_test, mcp_admission_test, mcp_route_census_test, mcp_catalog_truth_test, mcp_protocol_conformance_test = 36/36. Build freshness was checked first: every remediated module's .beam mtime is >= its source mtime, so the green result reflects current source and not a stale artifact.","T23-C02 (receipt hash collision) is CONFIRMED REMEDIATED by my own adversarial probe, not by the test suite alone. receipts.hash_payload now uses prefix-free framing `byte_size:field|` (receipts.gleam:40-57). I executed four collision attempts against the compiled module: Codex's exact pair hash_payload(\"tool\",[(\"a\",\"x&b=y\")]) vs [(\"a\",\"x\"),(\"b\",\"y\")] -> distinct; a framing-forgery pair [(\"a\",\"1:b|\")] vs [(\"a\",\"1\"),(\"b\",\"\")] -> distinct; a tool/field boundary-shift pair (\"too\",[(\"l\",\"x\")]) vs (\"tool\",[(\"\",\"x\")]) -> distinct; a duplicate-field pair -> distinct. All four returned false for equality.","T23-C01 (route grant ticket unvalidated) is CONFIRMED REMEDIATED and materially stronger than the required correction. verify_route_grant_ticket (route_convergence.gleam:114-153) now validates non-empty grant_id, revoked, expires_at_ms > now_ms, workspace_root equality, AuthorityScope sufficiency (AuditOnly refused outright; ControlActuation requires ControlMutation or ReleaseAuthority; Mutating refuses PublicReadOnly/ScopedObservation), and fence binding including grant_id match, TaskExecuting, worker/principal match and lease expiry. revoked_expired_or_foreign_route_grant_ticket_refused_test passes.","T23-C03 (private replay not owner-bound) is CONFIRMED REMEDIATED. dispatch.gleam:114-134 now derives an owner_hash over actor, workspace, plan_id, task_id, attempt and grant_id and prefixes it into intent_id, so two principals with byte-identical payloads no longer share a ledger entry. This is stricter than the public path, which binds actor+workspace+grant only. private_route_replay_isolated_across_principals_test passes.","Zenoh/HTTP transport asymmetry (my Round-1 observation) is CONFIRMED REMEDIATED. transports/zenoh.gleam:79-81 now uses crypto.hmac(..., crypto.Sha256, key) with domain separator \"uos-mcp-zenoh-v1:\" over the same length-prefixed framing, compares with crypto.secure_compare (line 210), and refuses a blank shared secret via string.trim == \"\" (line 183). zenoh_empty_secret_is_refused_test and malformed_zenoh_topic_has_no_unknown_fallback_test pass.","CLAIMED REMEDIATION THAT IS NON-FUNCTIONAL: verify_client_schema_pin (route_convergence.gleam:308-328) is DEAD CODE. I grepped all of src/ and test/: it has ZERO callers and ZERO tests. The live admission path still calls `registry.verify_schema_digest(spec, spec.input_schema)` at route_convergence.gleam:51 — a spec compared against its own schema, so SCHEMA_MIGRATION_REQUIRED remains structurally unreachable there, exactly as I reported in Round 1. The new function is correctly written (it compares a client-supplied Option(String) digest against spec.schema_digest and fails closed only when Some and mismatched), but because nothing calls it the Round-1 observation stands unremediated. No authority impact — the schema is server-owned, so there is nothing client-supplied to tamper with — but it must not be cited as schema-drift defence.","CRITICAL-CODE PIN IS PRESERVED, with a disambiguation worth recording: the SC-AGENTIC-RESILIENCE-001 pin at docs/sop/20261030-1058-...-system-prompt.md:184 covers harness/admission.gleam, which I re-hashed as c53ed6bf66cba56cfc0d6e850875a774 — unchanged and matching. The file the remediation modified is the DIFFERENT mcp/admission.gleam (md5 baf96be517d51ec5fcfce9116eefca83), which is not the pinned path. So no pin violation occurred, but the two same-named files are easy to conflate and Codex's Round-1 line \"the admission MD5 matches the requested value\" happens to be true of a file the remediation did not touch.","T21 disposition (item 7) CONFIRMED: the artifact governance/planning/20261030-1052-t21-workspace-and-head-disposition.json declares registered_workspaces_count 143 and visible_heads_count 147, I independently counted 143 workspace name entries, and mcp_workspace_disposition_test:251-297 parses the real file through the typed gate asserting 143/147 and preserving the SourceFX source4-test-2219 byte-for-byte hold. Codex's Round-1 caveat still holds and is not remediated by a count check: historical disposition labels do not independently prove content incorporation, which is why ancestry_without_content_disposition_is_not_integrated_test exists.","Doc truth claims (item 9) CONFIRMED against source. The rule now states protocol 2024-11-05 (Legacy20241105) and 2026-07-28 (Modern20260728), matching mcp/version.gleam:9-15 exactly and resolving Codex's Round-1 observation that the docs advertised 2025-11-25. The `65 declared - 12 unavailable = 53 advertised` reconciliation note matches tools.gleam:34-48 and is corroborated by mcp_catalog_truth_test (declared_stubs_are_not_operational_tools_test, capability_inventory_preserves_unprobed_boundary_test). HTTPS Tailscale Origin is real: http_auth.gleam:90 requires the https:// prefix, line 109 pins the tail55d152.ts.net label structure, and the header comment and code reject wildcard, IP-literal, credential and path forms.","CARRIED FORWARD UNREMEDIATED from my Round 1, unchanged and still minor: (a) registry.gleam:274 retains the `_ -> (\"unclassified\", ..., ReadOnly, ...)` fallback arm — unreachable for the current 65-name catalog, but it would silently classify any FUTURE public tool as ReadOnly and bypass the effect fence; prefer a total match or explicit refusal. (b) Two public entry points with different grant requirements persist: execute_authenticated (server.gleam:74) requires only the TaskFence GrantTicket, while execute_route_authenticated (server.gleam:110) additionally enforces route convergence; the hard fence still holds on both, so there is no escalation, but the API shape invites the weaker call path. (c) The 12 declared-unavailable tools remain dispatchable on the typed path — tools.unavailable_reason gates only tools/list (tools.gleam:47) and the legacy string dispatcher (server.gleam:740) — and their adapters refuse honestly, so the direction of the inconsistency remains safe.","REVIEW SCOPE LIMITS (reviewer-side, not candidate defects). First: tools/lean execution was DENIED in this session, so I did NOT personally re-execute any Lean or Quint row. Item 8 is receipt-bound only — I read docs/reviews/20261030-1155-t20-inproject-formal-gate-probe.json and confirmed it records in-project entrypoints (tools/lean -> toolchains/lean-4.33.0/bin/lean 4.33.0; tools/quint -> toolchains/nix-profile/bin/quint 0.32.0) with exit codes and digests, and that it honestly states the quint symlink resolves to a /nix/store path as the store boundary. I therefore set formal_and_oracle_gates_verified false. Second: Codex's Round-1 caveat that a Quint typecheck does not establish temporal-invariant verification is a property of the tool invocation, and the receipt's --invariant=reqClosed row plus negative control is the right shape, but I did not re-run it. Third: I executed no network, no Zenoh router and no live HTTP listener, so all transport evidence is in-process.","Round-1 artifact hygiene, non-blocking: docs/reviews/20261030-1145-claude-t23-final-dev-acceptance.json is wrapped in a ```json markdown fence, so it is not directly JSON-parseable; the Codex Round-1 file is clean. A pre-existing docs/reviews/20261030-1202-claude-t23-final-dev-acceptance-r2.json was present before I began; I did not read it as authority and derived this verdict from my own execution and source reading.","No files were modified, no commit, bookmark, release or admission authority was exercised, and no Sa-plan task state was changed by this review. Per SC-JIDOKA-001 and SC-SA-PLAN-001, Sa-plan remains the sole execution authority and this verdict grants no effect authority."],"matrix_summary":{"public_routes_verified":325,"development_routes_verified":105,"successor_routes_verified":75,"total_routes_verified":535,"stdio_cells_verified":505,"zenoh_cells_verified":15,"http_cells_verified":15,"positive_outcome_bound":true,"candidate_bound":false,"candidate_binding_note":"All 535 cells assert the constant literal \"uos-mcp\", and the live ledger uses